## .NET 8 Migration: `TimedExecutor`

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. Removed entirely.
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()`** — same, removed.
3. **`ThreadAbortException`** — can never be thrown; catching it is dead code.

### Why `Thread.Abort` Was Removed

It was always unsafe — it could corrupt state by interrupting code mid-execution (inside `finally` blocks, during I/O, in unmanaged calls). .NET Core replaced it with **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            if (task.Wait(timeout))
                return task.Result;

            // Signal cancellation (cooperative — work must observe it)
            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            // Unwrap single inner exception to match original behavior
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version with cooperative cancellation
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Critical Pitfall: Cancellation Is Cooperative

Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation **won't force-stop** a runaway delegate. The work must check the token:

```csharp
// ❌ Won't stop — ignores cancellation
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() => {
    while (true) { /* infinite loop */ }
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

// ✅ Cooperative — checks the token
await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(async (ct) => {
    while (!ct.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        await Task.Delay(100, ct);
        // do incremental work
    }
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

### Migration Checklist

| Concern | Action |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort` | Replace with `CancellationToken` |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Remove — never thrown |
| `Thread.ResetAbort` | Remove — never needed |
| Fire-and-forget threads | Use `Task.Run` with tokens |
| Truly uncooperative code | Isolate in a **separate process** and kill the process |
| Blocking callers | Offer an `async` overload |

### If You Must Kill Uncooperative Work

There is **no in-process equivalent** to `Thread.Abort`. If the work can't be made cooperative, run it out-of-process:

```csharp
using var process = Process.Start("worker.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("Worker process timed out");
}
```

This is the only safe way to forcibly terminate work in .NET 8+.
